# Val Ark - local configuration template
#
# Copy this to ".env" (which is git-ignored) and edit for your machine:
#     cp .env.example .env
#
# Nothing host-specific is ever committed; .env holds YOUR paths only.
# All values are optional — Val Ark autodetects sensible defaults if unset.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# DATA ROOT — the disk Val Ark fills and measures free space against.
# Models live at   $VAL_ARK_DATA/models   (reused if already present)
# Everything else  $VAL_ARK_DATA/val-ark/{tools,content,sources,assets,installers,state}
#
# If unset, Val Ark autodetects the largest writable mount (/mnt/*, /data, ...),
# falling back to the repo directory for single-disk / dev use.
# VAL_ARK_DATA=/mnt/bigdisk
# VAL_ARK_DATA=/srv/val-ark
# VAL_ARK_DATA=$HOME/val-ark-data

# Extra autodetect candidates (colon-separated), tried before the built-ins.
# VAL_ARK_DATA_CANDIDATES=/mnt/bigdisk:/data2

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# DISK RESERVE — never fill past this. Keeps the OS/disk healthy and leaves
# headroom for the "cut back for smaller valuable files" curation rule.
# Reserve = max(VALARK_RESERVE_PCT% of total, VALARK_RESERVE_MIN_GB).
# VALARK_RESERVE_PCT=2
# VALARK_RESERVE_MIN_GB=50

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# FINE-GRAINED OVERRIDES (rarely needed) — point individual trees elsewhere.
# VALARK_MODELS_DIR=/mnt/disk-a/models
# VALARK_TOOLS_DIR=/mnt/disk-b/tools
# VALARK_CONTENT_DIR=/mnt/disk-c/content

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WEB UI / API — port the server (scripts/server.js) listens on.
# Set this if 3000 is taken; scripts/loop.sh and verify.sh honor it too.
# VALARK_WEB_PORT=3000
# The web server only answers to names that can belong to this box (DNS-rebinding guard): IP
# addresses, localhost, single-label LAN names, *.local / *.lan / *.home / *.home.arpa / *.internal /
# *.localdomain / *.ts.net / *.fritz.box, VALARK_TLS_DOMAIN and the box's hostname. Add any other name you reach it by:
# VALARK_ALLOWED_HOSTS=ark.example.org,library.myhome
#
# Serve on a standard port too (http://<host>/ with no :3000). The loop keeps an
# idempotent iptables NAT redirect public->web port in place (all real
# interfaces: LAN + tailscale/VPN; loopback untouched, so an appliance's own
# 127.0.0.1:80 UI keeps working). Needs root or passwordless sudo for iptables.
# VALARK_WEB_PUBLIC_PORT=80
#
# Additional ports for the server to LISTEN on directly (comma-separated).
# Unlike the NAT redirect above this binds real sockets; ports <1024 need
#   sudo setcap 'cap_net_bind_service=+ep' $(command -v node)
# and a wildcard :80 bind fails if anything else holds 80 on ANY address.
# VALARK_WEB_EXTRA_PORTS=8080,8081
#
# Static browse pages (index.html + browse/ in the archive folder) for serving the
# archive from any plain file share, no server needed (docs/SERVING.md). `librarian.sh
# maintain` rewrites them at most every VALARK_STATIC_SITE_HOURS hours.
# VALARK_STATIC_SITE=0 turns them off.
# VALARK_STATIC_SITE=1
# VALARK_STATIC_SITE_HOURS=6

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CAD-AGENT REFERENCES — scripts/tools/cad-agent-references.sh archives every
# source cad-agent cites (repos as git bundles, pages, PDFs) into PRIVATE_DIR,
# which the web server never serves. OPT-IN: not part of `download-tools.sh all`;
# run the script yourself (its header says how). No repo size cap by default
# (~37 GB in all); set a cap in MB on small disks (larger repos keep a pointer only).
# VALARK_PRIVATE_DIR=/mnt/disk-a/val-ark/private
# PRIVATE_DIR, STATE_DIR and VALARK_SEAWEED_DIR are made owner-only (mode 700) every loop
# cycle (docs/SERVING.md): point them at a dedicated subfolder, never at a disk's mount root.
# VALARK_REFS_MAX_REPO_MB=250
# VALARK_REFS_REFRESH_DAYS=30
# VALARK_REFS_INDEX=https://cad-agent.valpatel.com/catalog/references/index.json

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# LASTBOOK — scripts/tools/lastbook.sh mirrors the current Lastbook release (free
# offline survival library: packs, state maps, address search, local AI model, app
# installers) into CONTENT_DIR/lastbook (served to the LAN). By default the
# librarian's planner sizes it (#203): each pack (Small 7.7 GB / Medium 67.5 GB /
# All 388 GB, nested: at most one is kept), state map (+ address index), the model
# and the installers compete with the ZIMs, models and installers on value per byte;
# the survival profile takes the Small pack + installers as essentials (fetched
# first; the Small is pruned once a larger planned pack verifies).
# In planner mode MAPS (none | CA,TX; auto/all = every map is OFFERED, the planner
# decides), ADDRESSES (follow | none), MODEL (no; yes/auto = offered) and APPS (none)
# filter what is offered, at once (even with a stale/offline catalog cache); a "no"
# also means a held item is not refreshed.
# Share mode (the planner stays out; sized by BUDGET_PCT, default 50): PACK other than
# auto (servers / NAS mirrors: PACK=every MAPS=all MODEL=yes, ~570 GB), an explicit
# BUDGET, PLAN=0, ADDRESSES=all or a code list, or a VALARK_LASTBOOK_DIR on another
# filesystem than the data root (no per-destination planner budget yet).
# VALARK_LASTBOOK_DIR=/mnt/disk-a/val-ark/content/lastbook
# VALARK_LASTBOOK_PACK=auto          # auto | small | medium | all | every | none | small,medium
# VALARK_LASTBOOK_MAPS=auto          # auto | all | none | CA,TX,...
# VALARK_LASTBOOK_ADDRESSES=follow   # follow (= the selected maps) | none; all | CA,TX,... = share mode
# VALARK_LASTBOOK_MODEL=auto         # auto | yes | no
# VALARK_LASTBOOK_APPS=current       # current | none
# VALARK_LASTBOOK_PLAN=1             # 0 = keep the planner out (size by the share below even with PACK=auto)
# VALARK_LASTBOOK_BUDGET=            # explicit bytes a run may add (-1 = no limit); keeps the planner out
# VALARK_LASTBOOK_BUDGET_PCT=50      # DEPRECATED (#203): the old fixed share, % of (headroom on its disk + what
#                                    #   it holds). Setting it keeps the planner out (and logs a warning); it is
#                                    #   still the share for PACK=every / an explicit pack.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# TOOL REFRESH — the loop re-runs the tool mirror every N days so mirrored apps
# track their latest upstream versions (0 = never). Each pass is bounded by
# VALARK_TOOL_REFRESH_TIMEOUT seconds and resumes next time if cut short.
# VALARK_TOOL_REFRESH_DAYS=7
# VALARK_TOOL_REFRESH_TIMEOUT=7200
# It runs BEFORE the top-up fill (loop step 5c; apps before more models, #347) and is
# skipped under the STOP flag or with under 1 GiB of budget (reserve / VALARK_MAX_GB).

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# DEV-STACK MIRROR (#347) — loop step 5d, before the model fill: the developer +
# security stack in data/dev-stack.tsv (Gitea builds, Caddy, Headscale, KeePassXC,
# compilers, Docker, ...; scripts/lib/release_mirror.py fetch) into
# <data>/val-ark/dev-stack. On by default; 0 turns it off. Bounded per run by the
# timeout and the librarian's budget (the reserve and VALARK_MAX_GB, which counts
# this tree); STOP and the reserve are re-checked every 256 MiB. A run cut short
# continues next cycle; a deferred or failed run retries in a day. Full stack ~130 GB
# (+ ~64 GB for the top-1000 PyPI wheelhouse). Set GITHUB_TOKEN (below): a run makes
# ~250 GitHub API calls and the anonymous limit is 60 an hour.
# VALARK_DEVSTACK=1
# VALARK_DEVSTACK_REFRESH_DAYS=7       # 0 = off too
# VALARK_DEVSTACK_TIMEOUT=3600         # seconds per run
# VALARK_DEVSTACK_MAX_GB=              # cap for the whole dev-stack tree (empty = only the fill's budget)
# VALARK_DEVSTACK_GROUPS=              # comma list, e.g. python,toolchains,security (empty = every group)
# VALARK_DEVSTACK_DIR=                 # default <data>/val-ark/dev-stack

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# STALE PARTIAL GC — failed librarian downloads keep their .part (+ aria2 .aria2
# control file) so multi-GB downloads resume across loop cycles instead of
# restarting from byte 0. A partial untouched for this many days (dead URL,
# catalog rotation) is deleted by `librarian.sh verify`. Active retries refresh
# the file every cycle, so only genuinely abandoned partials age out.
# VALARK_PARTIAL_MAX_AGE_DAYS=14

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ZIM LANGUAGES — which Kiwix ZIM languages the catalog includes (space-separated).
# Default: eng spa fra deu rus ara hin zho por
# VALARK_ZIM_LANGS="eng spa fra deu rus ara hin zho por"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# MODEL SCOUT — weekly Hugging Face search for new open models (docs/LIBRARIAN.md).
# VALARK_SCOUT=0 turns it off. Sweeps: speech-vision (speech + vision models <=6 GB,
# any age, high priority; default), small (new models <=10 GB; default) and frontier
# (big open-weight releases, filled last). TTL in seconds between scouts.
# VALARK_SCOUT=1
# VALARK_SCOUT_SWEEPS=small,speech-vision
# VALARK_SCOUT_TTL=604800
# VALARK_SCOUT_RETRY=86400
# VALARK_SCOUT_TIMEOUT=1800   # seconds; a scout that runs longer is dropped (cache kept)
# A run makes ~55 listings + up to ~350 repo lookups; HF_TOKEN (read-only) raises the
# Hugging Face rate limit. Changing the sweeps re-scouts on the next maintain.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# DIRECT-CONNECT LAPTOP: DHCP + DNS on the wired port (issue #148). Off by default.
# When a laptop is plugged straight into the Ark's ethernet port and NOTHING else on
# that link hands out addresses, the loop serves a tiny DHCP + DNS (dnsmasq) on that
# ONE interface: it gives itself <NET>.1/24, leases <NET>.10-60, and DNS answers the
# box's hostname (and "valark") with <NET>.1, so http://valark/ works with no router.
# It probes first (4 DISCOVERs over ~12 s) and serves only after two empty probes at
# least CONFIRM_SECS apart; it NEVER serves while a server answers. A watcher re-probes
# on every carrier change and every RECHECK_SECS and stops the moment a real server
# appears. No router option is handed out, so the laptop keeps its own default route.
# Needs root or passwordless sudo, plus dnsmasq (apt install dnsmasq-base); without
# either it logs one line and does nothing. Runtime files live in /run (tmpfs).
# VALARK_LINK_DHCP=1
# VALARK_LINK_DHCP_IFACE=eth0            # default: the ONE wired, non-wireless iface
# VALARK_LINK_DHCP_NET=10.254.77         # private /24 prefix (3 octets) it assigns itself
# VALARK_LINK_DHCP_RANGE_START=10        # lease range: <NET>.START .. <NET>.END
# VALARK_LINK_DHCP_RANGE_END=60
# VALARK_LINK_DHCP_LEASE=1h              # lease once settled ([0-9]+[mh])
# VALARK_LINK_DHCP_EARLY_LEASE=5m        # lease for the first EARLY_SECS of serving
# VALARK_LINK_DHCP_EARLY_SECS=600
# VALARK_LINK_DHCP_NAMES="ark nas"       # extra DNS names (letters, numbers, '-')
# DNS handed to the laptop:
#   forward (default) answer the box's names (+ <name>.home.arpa) and pass every
#           other lookup to the box's OWN upstream resolvers from /etc/resolv.conf,
#           so a laptop that also has Wi-Fi keeps its internet while the Ark is
#           online. Our own link address/subnet is never used as an upstream (no
#           self-forwarding loop); with no usable upstream (Ark offline) it acts
#           like local. A resolv.conf change restarts dnsmasq with the new list.
#           Privacy: the laptop's lookups then go out via the Ark's upstream.
#   local   answer only the box's names, REFUSE the rest (fails fast).
#   off     no DNS at all (open the UI by IP: http://<NET>.1/).
# VALARK_LINK_DHCP_DNS=forward
# VALARK_LINK_DHCP_PROBE_SECS=4          # listen time after the last DISCOVER
# VALARK_LINK_DHCP_CONFIRM_SECS=30       # gap between the two empty probes
# VALARK_LINK_DHCP_CONFIRM_MAX_SECS=300  # ...at most this far apart, else start over
# VALARK_LINK_DHCP_RECHECK_SECS=60       # re-probe interval while idle/serving
# VALARK_LINK_DHCP_WATCH_SECS=2          # carrier / carrier_changes poll interval

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# COMMUNITY SERVICES (chat, mail, message boards, files/pastebin). Val Ark can
# run these LAN-bound and frame them in the web shell at /app/<id>/. See
# docs/COMMUNITY.md. Mirror a service with scripts/tools/<id>.sh, then enable it
# here so the loop keeps it running. Empty = none enabled.
# VALARK_SERVICES="paste"
# VALARK_SERVICES="chat mail forum paste"
#
# Bind address for the community PROTOCOL ports only: mail IMAP/submission and native
# IRC. 0.0.0.0 = whole LAN; 127.0.0.1 = host-only. The sub-apps' web UIs (MicroBin,
# NodeBB, The Lounge, alps) always bind 127.0.0.1, whatever this says, so the LAN reaches
# them only through the Val Ark proxy (upload sandbox, read-wall; #317).
# VALARK_BIND=0.0.0.0
# The web server only shares the library with the LAN, the tailnet and the box itself
# (docs/design/access-identity.md). With VALARK_BIND=:: an IPv6 peer counts as LAN when it
# is link-local, ULA, or on a /64 (or longer) prefix of an interface that also has a private
# IPv4 address (a home LAN). On a cloud VM or an ISP's shared /64 they typically aren't trusted;
# note that on a shared cloud private network, RFC 1918 trust already includes other tenants.
#
# Per-service admin/login overrides (otherwise generated on first run):
# VALARK_CHAT_ADMIN_USER=admin
# VALARK_CHAT_ADMIN_PASS=change-me
# VALARK_FORUM_ADMIN_USERNAME=admin
# VALARK_FORUM_ADMIN_PASSWORD=change-me
# VALARK_FORUM_ADMIN_EMAIL=admin@valark.lan
# PASTE_AUTH_USER=valark
# PASTE_AUTH_PASSWORD=change-me
# PASTE_ADMIN_PASSWORD=change-me

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CONTENT MODERATION — the self-heal loop screens already-stored uploads with the
# box's own offline classifier and quarantines anything flagged into an admin
# review queue (Safety card). Screening is ON by default (fail-closed), but the
# loop's enforcement SWEEP only touches a PLAIN-FILE tree you explicitly point it
# at: with neither key set, `mod-sweep.sh` logs "no upload dirs to screen" and does
# nothing. It deliberately never sweeps a service's DB-backed store (MicroBin
# SQLite, maddy mail) — moving a DB-referenced file would corrupt the store.
#
# The dedicated uploads / drop area to sweep (one plain-file dir you serve or share):
# VAL_ARK_UPLOADS=$VAL_ARK_DATA/val-ark/uploads
#
# Explicit dirs to sweep (colon-separated); when set, overrides VAL_ARK_UPLOADS.
# VALARK_MODERATION_DIRS=/srv/uploads:/srv/dropbox
#
# Max files screened per sweep pass (bounds one cycle; the rest wait for the next).
# VALARK_MODERATION_SWEEP_MAX=200

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# OPTIONAL — raise GitHub API rate limits for link checks / release lookups.
# GITHUB_TOKEN=ghp_xxx

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# REMOTE FLEET for the verification loop (space-separated user@host or host).
# Leave empty to skip remote checks. mDNS .local names and user@ip both work.
# Put YOUR hosts here in .env (this template ships only placeholders).
# VALARK_FLEET="node-a.local node-b.local user@10.0.0.20"
#
# Where the fleet mounts the Val Ark content (for the "fleet sees content" check).
# Defaults to VAL_ARK_DATA if unset.
# VALARK_FLEET_DATA=/mnt/valark
